#### C. Update VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java
Inside your executeAsyncBatchCreation method, read the metadata and save it into the VoucherPosting record:
```java
@Async("bulkExecutor")
public void executeAsyncBatchCreation(String batchId, String journalPrefix, String requestId, String commonRemarks, String creatorId, Integer creatorRole) {
    
    // 1. READ FROM REDIS
    String scheduleStr = (String) redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("META_SCHEDULE_" + requestId);
    LocalDateTime scheduledTime = null;
    if (scheduleStr != null) {
        // Ensure format matches: "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"
        scheduledTime = LocalDateTime.parse(scheduleStr, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"));
    }

    // ... (Your validation & insertion logic) ...

    // 2. SAVE TO VOUCHER_POSTING
    VoucherPosting posting = new VoucherPosting();
    // ... (other fields)
    posting.setExecutionScheduled(scheduledTime != null ? "YES" : "NO");
    posting.setScheduledTime(scheduledTime);
    posting.setIsScheduleOverride("NO");
    
    voucherPostingRepository.save(posting);
    
    // ...
}

```
By passing the scheduledTime via the requestId metadata in Redis, you have created a **perfectly asynchronous, race-condition-free schedule** that will be populated the moment the batch is born in the database.
